-- Passage rg10-connections-p05
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('d166a02d-9a4c-5e2a-9bca-7e1045036d3f', 'rg10-connections-p05', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g10-connections'), null,
        '9-12', $q$The Ramps Everyone Uses$q$, $q$In the fictional city of Brannock, most street corners once ended in a sharp curb about fifteen centimeters high. For a person walking, the step was easy to ignore. For a person in a wheelchair, it was a wall.

In 1978, a group of residents led by Delphine Achebe began asking the city to replace curbs with gentle ramps. They gathered stories from neighbors who could not reach the library, the bank or the bus stop without help. The city council agreed to a small trial: ramps at forty corners near the central market.

The ramps did what Achebe's group had hoped, and wheelchair users began crossing the market district on their own. But the city's traffic counters noticed something nobody had planned for. Parents pushing strollers, delivery workers with hand trucks, travelers pulling suitcases and children on bicycles all began choosing the routes with ramps. A survey a year later found that nine out of ten people who used the ramps were not in wheelchairs.

That discovery changed the argument. The council had approved the trial as a service for a small group; now it saw the ramps as an improvement for nearly everyone. Within a decade, Brannock required ramps at every new or repaired corner.

The lesson spread beyond curbs. When Brannock's planners later designed bus stops, they added level boarding platforms first meant for riders with walkers, and soon noticed shorter boarding times for every passenger. Planners in the city now ask a single question at the start of each project: if a change helps the people who struggle most, who else might it help?$q$, 'draft')
