-- Passage rg10-connections-p06
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('fa102993-4ead-54d2-b6ef-7908e0743fbb', 'rg10-connections-p06', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g10-connections'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Finding a River in the Archives$q$, $q$In 2015, the fictional Halden County Library began scanning its collection of local newspapers, which stretched back to 1868. The goal was modest: to let family historians search old pages from home instead of turning brittle paper in the reading room.

The project ran into trouble almost at once. The software that turns scanned images into searchable text had been trained on modern print. Faced with faded ink and old-fashioned typefaces, it confused letters constantly, so a search for "Halden" might miss half the pages where the word appeared.

The library's archivist, Samir Okafor, asked the public for help. More than six hundred volunteers signed up to read scanned pages and correct the software's errors by hand. As the corrected text grew, searches became reliable, and new kinds of users arrived: students, local reporters and, unexpectedly, the county's engineers.

The engineers were planning a new drainage system for the town of Merriton, which flooded every few years for reasons no one fully understood. Searching the corrected papers, one engineer found an 1889 notice about a creek that had been filled in and covered by a street. Later articles described floods along that same street. The old creek bed, it turned out, still guided water underground toward the lowest part of town.

With that knowledge, the engineers redesigned the drainage plan to follow the buried creek instead of fighting it. Merriton has not flooded since the new system opened. The project that began as a convenience for family historians ended up solving a problem that had puzzled the town for over a century.$q$, 'draft')
