   $q$Why did Wexmoor's ice trade fail to recover even after cold winters returned?$q$, $q$[{"text":"The rail line to the cities closed, so Wexmoor had no way to ship its ice anymore.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"unsupported-inference"},{"text":"The icehouses had burned down, and the town could not afford to rebuild them.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"unsupported-inference"},{"text":"City buyers kept choosing factory ice because it arrived on schedule regardless of weather.","is_correct":true},{"text":"Lake Tamsin was turned into a skating area, so cutting ice there was no longer allowed.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"confuses-cause-effect"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$City buyers kept choosing factory ice because it arrived on schedule regardless of weather.$q$, $q$Paragraph 5 says buyers chose factory ice, "which arrived on schedule and did not depend on the weather," and then the icehouses closed. Skating is described as the lake's present use, a result of the trade ending rather than its cause.$q$, 'draft', '95f8d987-0aa8-5210-8647-ad01bca73e0d')
